	faithHeading="Faith and the Bible in Ukraine"
	faithBodyHtml={`<p>Ukraine&rsquo;s Christian story is long and public. The Razumkov Centre&rsquo;s November 2025 survey (2,009 adults, face to face, in government-controlled areas) found <strong>58% Orthodox</strong>, about 12% Greek Catholic, 3% Protestant, 1% Roman Catholic and 10% who call themselves simply &ldquo;Christian&rdquo; &mdash; roughly 84% in all &mdash; with 15.5% non-religious and 70% saying they are believers.</p><p>The Bible has never been far from that story. The Peresopnytsia Gospel (1556&ndash;1561) and the Ostrog Bible (1580&ndash;1581) were written and printed on Ukrainian soil; the first complete Bible in modern Ukrainian reached readers in 1903, and Ivan Ohiienko&rsquo;s translation followed in 1962.</p><p>What many Ukrainians now ask is not whether the Bible is theirs &mdash; it is how to hear, in plain and living words, a Father who is near. That is the quiet gift of Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; in Ukrainian.</p>`}
